body:
This is much more readable than before, thanks! 

I still find tests are even more readable when all that is needed for it is explicitly set in the test itself. Here, part of the knowledge is in the `beforeEach` (the fact that we set `nbPages: 30`).

What about putting a `paginationOptions = {container, scrollTo: false, cssClasses}` in the `beforeEach`, and then writing tests like:

```
// Given
paginationOptions.maxPages = 20;
results.nbPages = 30;

// When
var actual = pagination(paginationOptions).getMaxPage(results);

// Then
expect(actual).toEqual(20);
```

I wouldn't say this is blocking though, I just find it easier to understand the tests that way. We split it in the initial test state, the action, and the expectation. Any thoughts @vvo?

